WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.680
The first film showed what a plant builds its sugar from.

2
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.780
This film asks how fast it can build.

3
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.060
Why does more light stop speeding a leaf up?

4
00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:15.963
Here is a way to watch the rate.

5
00:00:17.263 --> 00:00:19.163
Punch small disks out of a spinach leaf.

6
00:00:20.463 --> 00:00:22.063
Pull the air out of them so they sink.

7
00:00:23.303 --> 00:00:25.343
Drop them into water with baking soda.

8
00:00:26.717 --> 00:00:29.617
Baking soda adds dissolved carbon dioxide.

9
00:00:30.917 --> 00:00:32.317
Now shine a lamp on the cup.

10
00:00:33.557 --> 00:00:37.537
As the disks photosynthesize, oxygen gas collects inside them.

11
00:00:38.617 --> 00:00:40.437
One by one, they float back up.

12
00:00:42.517 --> 00:00:44.837
The sooner they float, the faster the rate.

13
00:00:46.077 --> 00:00:48.237
Move the lamp closer, step by step.

14
00:00:49.437 --> 00:00:51.077
Each time, the disks float sooner.

15
00:00:52.377 --> 00:00:54.957
Plot the rate against brightness, and the curve climbs.

16
00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:58.690
This cup has only a pinch of baking soda.

17
00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.230
Keep moving the lamp closer.

18
00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:03.670
Does the curve keep climbing?

19
00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:06.090
Make your guess.

20
00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:16.877
It flattens. Brighter light now changes almost nothing.

21
00:01:18.217 --> 00:01:21.297
The disks are running short of carbon dioxide to build with.

22
00:01:22.637 --> 00:01:27.237
Carbon dioxide has become the slow worker, and the whole process waits for it.

23
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:33.120
An input or condition that holds a process back is called a limiting factor.

24
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.780
On the rising part of this curve, it was light.

25
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.180
On the flat part, it is carbon dioxide.

26
00:01:41.383 --> 00:01:42.983
So add more baking soda.

27
00:01:44.343 --> 00:01:46.743
Now the curve climbs higher before it flattens again.

28
00:01:48.123 --> 00:01:52.363
Something else, such as temperature or enzyme speed, now sets a new ceiling.

29
00:01:53.743 --> 00:01:57.283
When a curve is flat, more of that factor barely changes the rate.

30
00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:00.667
Now leave the lab for the Chesapeake Bay.

31
00:02:02.047 --> 00:02:05.267
Underwater grasses grow on the bottom of its shallow water.

32
00:02:06.547 --> 00:02:10.067
Most eelgrass lives in the lower Bay, where the water is saltier.

33
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.260
Light has to pass down through the water to reach the grass.

34
00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.480
Mud and floating algae block it.

35
00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.840
So does material growing on the leaves themselves.

36
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.030
For the saltier Bay, there is a habitat target.

37
00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.250
More than 22 percent of the light at the surface should reach the grass.

38
00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:35.230
When murky water cuts the light, light can become the limiting factor.

39
00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:41.493
In this cup, with the other conditions held fixed, carbon dioxide capped the rate.

40
00:02:42.793 --> 00:02:45.193
In the Bay, murky water can make light the limit.

41
00:02:46.613 --> 00:02:48.733
Real plants can face two limits at once.

42
00:02:49.973 --> 00:02:53.833
A flat curve means more of that factor is no longer helping much.
